Power plant leaders need information that helps them decide where to look, which questions to ask and who needs to act. An AI-enabled EHS dashboard should bring that information into view.
What should a power plant EHS dashboard show?
An AI-enabled EHS dashboard for power plants should show critical risks, control-verification status, high-risk work, occupational health priorities, significant incidents, overdue actions and data quality. Each priority should link to its location, evidence, responsible person and next step. AI insights should explain their basis and limitations.

That gives leaders a practical starting point for reviewing health and safety across the plant.

Begin with decisions, then choose the indicators
It is tempting to start a dashboard project by listing every available metric. A better starting point is the decision each metric should support.

A plant manager may need to know which unresolved concerns require resources. An outage manager may need to review overlapping activities. An EHS leader may need to check whether repeated findings indicate an ineffective control.

Those questions require different views.

A useful dashboard can provide a common overview while allowing authorised users to examine the underlying records. Every chart should help answer a question someone is responsible for addressing.

Eight areas leaders should be able to review
1. Critical risks and the status of their controls
The first view should identify significant unresolved concerns and the controls associated with them.

Depending on the plant and activity, these could involve electrical energy, pressure systems, confined spaces, lifting operations, work at height or hazardous substances.

For each priority, leaders should be able to see:

The affected equipment, area or activity.

The potential consequence.

The relevant control and its responsible owner.

The latest verification result.

Any outstanding action or review.

A completed inspection count is useful, but it does not establish that a particular critical control is effective.

HSE’s guidance on developing process safety indicators offers a framework for monitoring control over major-hazard risks. Its principles can inform a plant-specific approach.

2. High-risk work and coordination needs
A total number of permits provides limited context. Leaders also need to understand the activities behind that number.

A useful view can show work awaiting authorisation, suspended activities and outstanding checks, according to the site’s configured workflow. Location and timing help reviewers identify activities that may require coordination.

NeoEHS Permit to Work software supports digital work-authorisation processes.

The dashboard should show recorded status clearly. It must not imply that a permit approval, on its own, proves that physical conditions remain safe.

3. Occupational health priorities
Worker health deserves a visible place alongside incident statistics.

A leadership view could include the status of exposure assessments, planned monitoring, health-related corrective actions and aggregate completion of required occupational health activities.

For example, a team might need to review overdue noise assessments or follow-up actions associated with dust, heat or chemical exposure. The relevant priorities depend on the plant’s assessed risks.

Keep individual medical information restricted to appropriately authorised professionals. A general management dashboard should show the operational follow-up leaders need without exposing diagnoses or confidential clinical records.

These are dashboard design requirements to assess during implementation—not a claim that every health workflow is included by default.

4. Corrective actions that remain unresolved—or keep returning
An overall closure percentage can hide important differences.

Several minor actions may be completed while one significant concern remains open. An action may also be closed administratively without checking whether the response worked.

Leaders should be able to review actions by priority, age, owner and affected activity. Reopened actions and recurring findings deserve particular attention.

Useful questions include:

Which priority actions are overdue?

Which need resources or a management decision?

Which have completion evidence awaiting verification?

Where has the same concern returned?

