6. Leading indicators alongside historical outcomes
Leading indicators examine activities and conditions that can help reveal developing problems. Lagging indicators describe outcomes that have already occurred.

OSHA’s leading-indicator guidance encourages using both perspectives in a safety and health programme.

For a power plant, a balanced review might include control-check completion, response times, repeat findings and overdue actions alongside injuries and other recorded events.

Each measure needs a clear definition, reporting period and denominator.

For example:

On-time verification rate = checks completed by their due time ÷ checks due in the period × 100.

Also show how many completed checks found a control ineffective. Otherwise, a high completion rate could be mistaken for evidence that all controls passed.

7. AI insights that can be examined
AI can help group similar observations, identify recurring themes and draft summaries of records for review. Predictive models may also support prioritisation where suitable data and validation are available.

The dashboard should explain:

Which records contributed to the insight.

The period and locations covered.

Why the issue was highlighted.

Whether information is missing.

Who has reviewed the output.

A generated explanation is not proof of causation. Nor should a risk score be presented as a calibrated probability unless that interpretation has been validated.

