8. Data freshness and coverage
A dashboard should make uncertainty visible.

When was the information captured? When was it synchronised? Are all departments included? Are contractor records complete? Is an integration delayed?

Distinguish a confirmed zero from missing data. An area with no submitted observations should not automatically appear green.

Where information is stale or incomplete, label it clearly. Leaders need to know when they are reviewing a partial picture.

A practical dashboard structure
A useful design can have three levels:

First: decisions requiring attention.
Significant unresolved concerns, failed control checks and actions needing leadership support.

Second: patterns and performance.
Trends in findings, occupational health follow-up, action effectiveness and incident learning.

Third: supporting evidence.
Source records, timestamps, photographs, approvals and verification details, subject to access permissions.

This gives leaders a manageable overview with a route to the evidence behind it.

The EHS dashboard supports management review. It does not replace plant alarms, DCS, SCADA or safety instrumented systems.

An example: when a positive headline hides a concern
This is an illustrative scenario, not a customer case study.

A plant reports that most planned inspections have been completed. The headline indicator looks positive.

A closer view shows that several findings relate to the same maintenance area. One action has been reopened, and a control verification is overdue before further work is planned.

The dashboard brings those records together. The responsible manager reviews the evidence, coordinates with the relevant teams and ensures decisions follow the site’s work-control procedures.

The useful outcome is a clearer decision based on connected information—not the colour of the headline indicator.

Avoid misleading comparisons
Comparing units or contractors requires context.

Raw incident counts can reflect different workforce sizes, hours worked, activities and reporting practices. A rise in observations may indicate stronger participation rather than worsening conditions.

Show definitions, scope and exposure denominators where relevant. Explain exclusions and changes in methodology.

The dashboard should support learning and accountability without encouraging teams to suppress reports to improve their appearance.

