Frequently asked questions
Which indicators belong on the first screen?
Prioritise significant unresolved risks, control-verification failures, high-risk work exceptions and overdue actions needing intervention. Include enough context to identify the owner and next step.

Are low injury rates enough to show that a plant is safe?
No. Injury rates describe recorded outcomes. Leaders also need evidence about current controls, exposures, work conditions and unresolved concerns.

What makes an EHS dashboard AI-enabled?
AI may analyse report text, identify patterns or generate summaries and model-based estimates. Charts, reminders and fixed thresholds can operate without AI.

Should leaders see individual employee health records?
A general leadership dashboard should normally show aggregate health-programme information and necessary operational actions. Access to identifiable medical information requires appropriate authorisation and safeguards.

How often should the dashboard update?
Set refresh requirements according to the decision and data source. Work-coordination records may require timely updates, while other reviews use periodic data. Display timestamps and delays clearly.

Can one overall safety score replace detailed indicators?
No. A combined score can hide a failed critical control or missing information. Keep significant exceptions visible and explain how any composite score is calculated.

Make the dashboard useful in the next meeting
Before adding another chart, ask: What decision will this help someone make?

A well-designed EHS dashboard helps power plant leaders identify concerns, examine the evidence and make responsibility clear. AI adds value when it makes that review more useful and understandable.

